  {
   "_id": 11419,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"2008\" data-start=\"1821\">Symbolic programming allows manipulation of algebraic structures without numerical approximation.<br data-end=\"2782\" data-start=\"2779\" />\nUsing Sympy in Python:</p>\n\n<p>from sympy import symbols, expand, factor</p>\n\n<p>x = symbols(&#39;x&#39;)<br />\nexpr = (x + 2)**2<br />\nexpanded = expand(expr)<br />\nfactored = factor(expanded)</p>\n\n<p>print(expanded) &nbsp;# x**2 + 4*x + 4<br />\nprint(factored) &nbsp;# (x + 2)**2</p>\n\n<p>This retains exact forms, enables simplification, factoring, and substitution. Unlike numerical methods, symbolic computation ensures algebraic integrity, which is vital in proof-based tasks or when exact forms are required (e.g., calculus, theorem solving).</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC203P",
